{
  "image_name": "bb537cdab18ee68799bcc1d9cbf06915.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic of a rechargeable aqueous Zn–MnO₂ battery, showing how the chemistry changes during discharge and charge. The zinc anode is on the left, the MnO₂ cathode on the right, and the electrolyte is a ZnSO₄/MnSO₄ solution in between. At the top, arrows indicate opposite electron flow directions for discharge and charge.\n\nDuring discharge, zinc metal at the anode is oxidized to Zn²⁺, releasing electrons, while MnO₂ at the cathode is reduced to MnOOH. Water dissociation appears to supply both OH⁻ and H⁺, with H⁺ participating near the cathode and OH⁻ building up in the electrolyte. These ions then combine with Zn²⁺ and sulfate to form a zinc hydroxide sulfate hydrate phase, labeled as ZnSO₄(Zn(OH)₂)₃·xH₂O, which deposits near the cathode/electrolyte region.\n\nDuring charge, the arrows reverse: MnOOH is converted back to MnO₂, the zinc-containing byproduct dissolves, and Zn²⁺ is reduced back to metallic Zn at the anode. Overall, the image emphasizes the coupled electron transfer, ion transport, proton/hydroxide involvement, and reversible formation/decomposition of the zinc hydroxide sulfate intermediate that accompanies cycling.",
